<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Since API 28, cleartext (http://) traffic is blocked by default. This app
  deliberately makes cleartext requests to two kinds of endpoints — the
  optional remote image-generation server (Settings → Image generation →
  Server URL, e.g. http://192.168.1.10:9100) and the pre-existing Hermes
  agent / Ollama endpoints — both of which are the user's own LAN
  infrastructure, typed in by hand as a raw IP (or "localhost"), never a
  hostname we control.

  Why base-config instead of domain-config: Android's <domain> element only
  matches an exact hostname/IP string (with includeSubdomains only expanding
  DNS subdomains) — it does not support CIDR ranges or wildcards for IP
  literals. There is no way to write "any RFC 1918 address" as a finite list
  of <domain> entries, and the user can type any of ~17.9M private-range
  addresses depending on their router's subnet. Enumerating exact IPs isn't
  practical for a "paste your LAN server's address" feature, so we fall back
  to base-config.

  This does mean cleartext is technically permitted app-wide, not just to
  private ranges — but nothing in this codebase issues an http:// request
  except these two user-configured, user-typed endpoints; every other
  network call in the app uses https:// by construction. So in practice this
  only ever affects same-LAN traffic the user explicitly opted into by
  typing an http:// URL into Settings.
-->
<network-security-config>
    <base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="true" />
</network-security-config>
